<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hoa Lư</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/968">968</a>–1010</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Hoa Lu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hoa Lư was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> from 968 to 1010, in present-day Ninh Bình province. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinh-tien-hoang">Đinh Tiên Hoàng</a> built it as a fortified capital among limestone hills, and the Early Lê kept it. In 1010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ly-thai-to">Lý Thái Tổ</a> moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thăng Long</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>; temples to the Đinh and Lê kings still stand at the site.</p></div>
